DAY 13 – NUMBER PROGRAMS USING LOOPS

1. NUMBER PROGRAMS USING LOOPS

Number programs can be solved using loops and basic
digit-processing operations.

Common number programs include:

• Reverse a number
• Palindrome
• Sum of digits
• Count even digits
• Armstrong number
• Factors of a number
• Count number of factors
• Factorial of a number


2. IMPORTANT NUMBER-PROGRAM OPERATIONS

n % 10

Gets the last digit of a number.

n // 10

Removes the last digit of a number.

n % i == 0

Checks whether i is a factor of n.

count = count + 1

Used to count something.


3. DIGIT-BASED PROGRAM FLOW

Read the number.

Store the original number if required.

Process the number while n > 0.

Get the last digit.

Process the digit.

Remove the last digit.

Repeat until the number becomes 0.


4. REVERSE A NUMBER

Reversing a number means arranging its digits
in the opposite order.

The last digit is obtained using % 10.

The last digit is removed using // 10.


5. PALINDROME NUMBER

A palindrome number remains the same when its digits
are reversed.

The original number is compared with its reversed number.


6. SUM OF DIGITS

Sum of digits means adding all the digits
of a number.

Each digit can be extracted using % 10.


7. COUNT EVEN DIGITS

Even digits are digits divisible by 2.

Condition:

digit % 2 == 0

A counter can be used to count the even digits.


8. FACTORS OF A NUMBER

A factor is a number that divides another number
without leaving a remainder.

Condition:

n % i == 0


9. COUNT NUMBER OF FACTORS

Instead of printing the factors, a counter can be
used to count the total number of factors.

The counter increases whenever a factor is found.


10. FACTORIAL OF A NUMBER

Factorial means multiplying a number by all
positive numbers below it.

Factorial is represented using !.

For a positive number n:

n! = n × (n - 1) × ... × 1


11. ARMSTRONG NUMBER

For a 3-digit number, if the sum of the cubes
of its digits is equal to the original number,
it is an Armstrong number.


12. CONTINUE STATEMENT

The continue statement skips the current iteration
and moves to the next iteration of the loop.


13. BREAK VS CONTINUE

continue → Skips the current iteration.

break → Stops the entire loop.

pass → Does nothing.


